"},"name":"Glob","type":"tool_use"},{"input":{"path":"/Users/blaise/Developer/cms/templates/astro-marketing/src/components/sections/Cta.astro"},"name":"Read","type":"tool_use"},{"input":{"path":"/Users/blaise/Developer/cms/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-29-orso-lovable-builder-design.md","offset":130,"limit":40},"name":"Read","type":"tool_use"},{"input":{"path":"/Users/blaise/Developer/cms/packages/astro/src/marketing.js","limit":80},"name":"Read","type":"tool_use"},{"input":{"pattern":"required|frontmatter|Fields","path":"/Users/blaise/Developer/cms/docs","glob":"**/*content*","head_limit":30},"name":"Grep","type":"tool_use"},{"text":"In the current marketing template, a CTA is **not** part of the markdown. Landing CTAs live in `src/components/sections/Cta.astro` and are pulled into `index.astro`. Blog/help `.md` files only have their own schemas (`title`, `description`, etc.) — no CTA field.\n\nIf you want a **required CTA on a markdown entry**, put it in that collection’s Zod schema as required frontmatter, then fill it in the Fields panel (or YAML):\n\n```ts\ncta: z.object({\n  heading: z.string(),\n  label: z.string(),\n  href: z.string(),\n}),\n```\n\n```yaml\n---\ntitle: Launch day\ncta:\n  heading: Ready to launch?\n  label: Get started\n  href: /pricing\n---\n```\n\nOrso treats that like any other required field: it shows in **Fields** with `*`, and save fails until it’s set. The markdown **body** stays prose; the CTA data stays in frontmatter (or a section variant if you use a discriminated `section` / `sections` union).\n\nSo: required CTA in markdown → schema + frontmatter. Landing-page CTA in this template → edit the Astro section, not a `.md` file.","type":"text"}]}
{"v":1,"agent":"cursor","cli_version":"0.12.2","type":"user","content":[{"text":"The CTA can be at the top middle or any way"}]}
{"v":1,"agent":"cursor","cli_version":"0.12.2","type":"assistant","content":[{"text":"If the CTA can sit at the top, middle, or anywhere, don’t model it as one fixed page field. Model it as an **ordered section** so position comes from order, not from a special “CTA slot.”\n\n**Page sections (landing-style)**  \nStore an ordered list and let `cta` be one variant:\n\n```yaml\n---\ntitle: Home\nsections:\n  - type: hero\n    heading: …\n  - type: cta\n    label: Get started\n    href: /pricing\n  - type: features\n    …\n---\n```\n\nOr separate section entries with `order`. The page just renders `sections` in order — CTA wherever it appears (first, middle, last, or more than once).\n\n**Inside an article body**  \nUse MDX and drop the component inline:\n\n```mdx\nIntro paragraph…\n\n\u003cCta heading=\"Ready?\" label=\"Get started\" href=\"/pricing\" /\u003e\n\nMore prose…\n```\n\nThat puts the CTA anywhere in the reading flow.\n\n**Rule of thumb:** fixed footer CTA → one frontmatter object or a hardcoded Astro section. CTA that can move → ordered sections or an inline MDX component, not a single required top-level `cta` field.","type":"text"}]}
